---
code: redir-target-top
when-fires: a write-redirect (`>`/`>>`) to a dynamic/unresolved target joins ⊤. effect.rs classify Redir arm (né dq-redir-target-top).
why: y-1 / 21F imp-1: the target is unresolvable so no per-path `file` cell can be keyed. NOTE plain-language pass owed: ⊤/top wording (law-plain-language-surfaces, 24H ack-4).
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# The redirect target comes from the environment, so no `file` cell names it.
hork --emit >"$LOG_DEST"

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
classify: note[redir-target-top]: This write-redirect's target cannot be
resolved statically, so the write cannot be tied to any one file and the command
always runs (never elided) -- a literal target, or a variable assigned from one,
would let Dorc track the write.
      [ book.sh:3:13
   3 | hork --emit >"$LOG_DEST"
     |             \__________/
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `hork --emit >"$LOG_DEST"` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site
detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:3:1
   3 | hork --emit >"$LOG_DEST"
     | \______________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `hork --emit >"$LOG_DEST"`
hint: 'hork' (line 3) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=1 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=1 may-alias=0
